      "vulnerable_function": "init(address), sweep(address,address)",
      "location": "BuilderWallet.init / BuilderWallet.sweep",
      "explanation": "BuilderWallet is intended to be a deterministic fee-recipient wallet whose sweeping power is controlled by the designated builder admin set by the BuilderFactory during deployment. However, BuilderWallet.init(address) is external and has no access control and no one-time initialization guard. Any address can call init at any time after deployment and overwrite builderAdmin. Since sweep() authorizes solely by msg.sender == builderAdmin, an attacker can take over any deployed BuilderWallet and drain all ERC20 balances held by it (i.e., builder fee revenue). This does not require compromising OWNER/guardian/admin keys and is fully permissionless once the wallet exists.",
      "attack_scenario": "1) A BuilderWallet is deployed via BuilderFactory.deployBuilder (or already exists) and later accumulates protocol fee tokens. 2) Attacker calls BuilderWallet(wallet).init(attackerAddress), overwriting builderAdmin. 3) Attacker calls BuilderWallet(wallet).sweep(token, attackerAddress) for each token held. 4) Wallet transfers its full balances to attacker.",
      "suggested_fix": "Restrict init to the factory and make it one-time:\n- `function init(address _builderAdmin) external { require(msg.sender == FACTORY, \"NOT_FACTORY\"); require(builderAdmin == address(0), \"ALREADY_INIT\"); require(_builderAdmin != address(0), \"ZERO_ADMIN\"); builderAdmin = _builderAdmin; }`\nOptionally set builderAdmin in the constructor (pass as constructor arg) to remove init entirely."
    }
  ],
  "overall_explanation": "Most of RiskEngine is computation-only (view/pure) and doesn’t custody funds; its main privileged actions are guarded by the immutable GUARDIAN. The concrete, exploitable issue is in the BuilderWallet fee recipient: its admin can be overwritten by anyone via an unprotected init(), enabling theft of all fees accrued to builder wallets."
